```java
// Legacy, can't be changed
interface LegacyPaymentGateway { int submitCharge(String cardNum, long cents); }

// What the rest of the app now expects
interface PaymentProvider { PaymentResult charge(Order order); }

class LegacyGatewayAdapter implements PaymentProvider {
    private final LegacyPaymentGateway legacy;
    LegacyGatewayAdapter(LegacyPaymentGateway legacy) { this.legacy = legacy; }
    public PaymentResult charge(Order order) {
        int code = legacy.submitCharge(order.getCardNumber(), order.getAmountCents());
        return code == 0 ? PaymentResult.success() : PaymentResult.failure(code);
    }
}
```

The [Adapter](adapter-pattern) translates shapes only — it doesn't change what either side does, just how they talk to each other.

```java
class PaymentFacade {
    private final PaymentProvider provider;
    PaymentFacade(PaymentProvider provider) { this.provider = provider; }

    PaymentResult processPayment(Order order) {
        log.info("charging order {}", order.getId());
        for (int attempt = 0; attempt < 3; attempt++) {
            PaymentResult result = provider.charge(order);
            if (result.isSuccess() || !result.isRetryable()) return result;
        }
        return PaymentResult.failure(-1);
    }
}
```

Callers now call one method, `processPayment()`, and the [Facade](facade-pattern) hides retries and logging behind it — regardless of whether `provider` underneath is the adapted legacy gateway or a brand-new one.
